[size=5][b]POI Map Plus[/b][/size]

[size=4][b]Description[/b][/size]

You clear a POI, drive on, and three days later you are standing in front of the same building wondering whether you have already stripped it. POI Map Plus answers that from the map screen.

Every POI you have been to gets a skull marker. Orange means its best container is still untouched. Green means someone already took it. Point at a marker and a tooltip gives you the POI name and its difficulty tier - no permanent labels cluttering the map, nothing revealed about places you have never approached.

It is a small quality of life mod: no new blocks, no new items, no balance changes. It only reads what the game already knows and puts it on the map.

[size=4][b]Main features[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]Markers for POIs you have visited.[/b] A marker appears the first time you get close enough for that part of the map to uncover, and stays from then on.
[*][b]Difficulty on hover.[/b] Name and tier in a tooltip.
[*][b]Looted or not, at a glance.[/b] Orange skull while the good loot is still there, green once it is gone.
[*][b]Follows the best container present.[/b] The mod ranks loot containers and watches the richest one a POI actually has, so a tier 5 chest is what counts in a tier 5 POI, and a weapons bag is what counts where that is all there is.
[*][b]Quest resets are respected.[/b] Reset a POI through a trader quest and its marker goes back to orange.
[*][b]Works on existing saves.[/b]
[/list]

[size=4][b]How "cleared" is decided[/b][/size]

The mod follows a ranked list of loot containers, from the richest down. In each POI it picks the best ranked container that is actually there and watches that one; lower ranks only matter when nothing better exists in that POI. An entry can be limited to small POIs - a weapons bag is the prize of a tier 1 house, but in a tier 5 dungeon it means nothing, so by default it only counts in tier 1.

Trash bins, mailboxes and other filler are not on the list at all, so opening a nightstand on the way in does not mark anything. A POI holding nothing from the list is marked cleared straight away. The full list sits in PoiMapPlus.xml and can be reordered or extended.

[size=4][b]Requirements[/b][/size]

[list]
[*]7 Days to Die [b]V 3.2.0+[/b]
[*]The game must run [b]without EasyAntiCheat[/b]. The mod ships a DLL and EAC blocks those.
[/list]

[size=4][b]Installation instructions[/b][/size]

[list=1]
[*]Download the archive (or install via Vortex).
[*]Extract it into your Mods folder so that you end up with [b]Mods\PoiMapPlus\ModInfo.xml[/b]. If your game has no Mods folder yet, create one next to 7DaysToDie.exe.
[*]Start the game without EasyAntiCheat.
[/list]

Vortex and the 7D2D Mod Launcher handle the archive as a normal modlet. To uninstall, delete the folder - the mod only ever writes its own small state file into your save.

[size=4][b]Multiplayer[/b][/size]

The mod is client side only: the server does not need it and neither do the other players. Everything it reads is already on your client, and looted marks live in your own save.

[b]That said, it has only been tested in single player.[/b] It should behave the same as a client on a dedicated server, but nobody has verified that yet - feedback welcome. Marks are personal: they do not sync with what your teammates have looted.

[size=4][b]Compatibility[/b][/size]

Hooks vanilla methods with Harmony postfixes and appends a single nav object class through XPath, so no vanilla config file is overwritten. UI overhauls that only restyle the map are fine; a mod that replaces the map controller with its own class would leave the markers unhooked. Containers added by other mods are supported - put their loot lists into the config. Large overhauls such as Darkness Falls or Undead Legacy rework loot completely, so the default ranking needs adjusting for them.

[size=4][b]Configuration[/b][/size]

Everything lives in [b]PoiMapPlus.xml[/b] next to ModInfo.xml, with comments explaining each setting. The ones people usually touch:

[list]
[*][b]MinTier[/b] - lowest POI tier that gets a marker. Default 1, which skips tier 0 sheds and ruined booths. Set it to 0 to see everything.
[*][b]OnlyDiscovered[/b] - set to false to reveal every POI in the world from the start.
[*][b]TopContainerCount[/b] - how many of the best containers must be looted before a POI counts as cleared. Default 1.
[*][b]tracked_loot_lists[/b] - which containers count and in what order.
[/list]

